// tap-echo: --buffer 65535 --close-after 0
// Ensure that the reception of a TCP FIN-ACK with SEG.SEQ = RCV.NXT-1
// (not acceptable) in the TIME-WAIT state results in sending an ACK with
// SEG.SEQ = SND.NXT and SEG.ACK = RCV.NXT.
// Adapted from freebsd-net/tcp-testsuite commit 04b8c73fc5081fe3792f1bed9168c9b22aaedddb,
// state-event-engine/rcv-fin-ack-time-wait/rcv-fin-ack-time-wait-using-shutdown-outside-left-ipv4.pkt
// (BSD-2-Clause; notice below). Changes for Zhida's packet-only runner, tap-echo
// being the application (fuzz/packetdrill/README.md, "Upstream suites"):
// - IPv4 variant. Dropped: sysctls, which set FreeBSD defaults; fcntl;
//   getsockopt(SO_RCVBUF); --tolerance_usecs=75000 (the runner keeps packetdrill's 4
//   ms); sockstat and tcpdrop commands except where replaced below.
// - Upstream reaches the state by an active open (connect; FreeBSD's SYN, the
//   peer's SYN-ACK <mss 1460,sackOK>, FreeBSD's ACK). tap-echo only listens, so the
//   client opens instead: the client's SYN carries the options upstream's peer sent
//   (same negotiated result: MSS 1460, SACK, no window scale, no timestamps), as in
//   upstream's own passive-open scripts (rcv-rst-syn-rcvd via-listen), then Zhida's
//   SYN-ACK and the client's ACK 0.1 s later.
// - shutdown(SHUT_WR) becomes tap-echo --close-after 0: Zhida sends its FIN right
//   after the handshake (upstream: 0.1 s later).
// - Receive window: tap-echo --buffer 65535, so Zhida's RCV.WND is 65535, its
//   advertised window (no window scale), as upstream's.
// - Still in TIME-WAIT (upstream: sockstat 0.1 s after the event): 1.1 s after the
//   event the client retransmits its FIN and Zhida ACKs it; a closed connection
//   would leave a listener, which answers RST. 1.1 s, not 0.1 s: Zhida sends at
//   most one ACK per second to out-of-window segments without payload (smoltcp
//   0.14.0 src/socket/tcp.rs 1545-1550, 1821-1830; Linux limits them to one per
//   0.5 s), and the event may have drawn one.
//
